0.- Strait of Hormuz = 
is the world's most critical maritime chokepoint for global energy and trade. Located between Iran and Oman, it connects the Persian Gulf to the Gulf of Oman and open ocean routes, carrying roughly one-fifth of the world's petroleum consumption and major liquefied natural gas (LNG) shipments.

Global Energy Supply

• Oil transit: Around 17 to 21 million barrels of oil pass through the strait daily, making it vital for producers like Saudi Arabia, Iraq, the UAE, Kuwait, Qatar, and Iran. The U.S. Energy Information Administration highlights how transit disruptions at this chokepoint can cause supply delays and higher shipping costs.
• LNG and markets: Large volumes of LNG and energy exports also move through the corridor, with Asian economies (such as China, India, and Japan) heavily reliant on these flows alongside buyers in Europe and elsewhere.

Narrow Geography and Limited Alternatives

• Tight lanes: At its narrowest point, the strait is only about 21 miles wide, with shipping lanes restricted to narrow corridors.
• Few bypass options: While a few pipelines can divert some oil around the strait, most Persian Gulf energy and commercial cargo still depend on this passage.

Broader Trade and Geopolitical Impact

• Supply chains: Beyond crude oil and LNG, container ships and vessels carrying manufactured goods, petrochemicals, food, and raw materials use the route. Users agree on r/oil on Reddit that the strait's importance extends past crude oil to broader global supply chains such as LNG, fertilizer, food, and industrial production.
• Economic shockwaves: Threats, naval tensions, or disruptions in the strait can spike oil prices, slow shipping, raise freight and manufacturing costs, and fuel global inflation. The Strauss Center notes its geo-strategic importance and the severe global economic risk of a major closure.



