3. Panama Canal-

The Panama Canal is vital because it provides a fast, cost-effective waterway between the Atlantic and Pacific oceans, saving ships roughly 8,000 nautical miles and weeks of travel compared to sailing around South America.


Key Reasons It Matters

• Global trade hub: It carries roughly 5% to 6% of global maritime trade—about $270 billion in cargo each year—across more than 12,000 to 14,000 ship transits.
• U.S. commerce and security: The United States is the canal's largest user, with around 40% of U.S. container traffic passing through it and naval vessels relying on it to move between oceans.
• Lower shipping costs: Shorter routes reduce fuel burn and transit times for goods like grains, oil, electronics, and manufactured products.
• Panama's economy: Transit tolls generate billions of dollars in revenue that support Panama's public budget and infrastructure.

Ongoing Challenges

• Droughts and water supply: Each transit uses around 52 million gallons of fresh water through the lock system, making operations vulnerable to low lake levels during dry spells.
• Geopolitical tension: Because it is a major shipping choke point, control, port investments, and transit access remain frequent points of international scrutiny.
For a breakdown of its trade numbers and strategic role, see Northern Trust, or read PBS News on how it shaped U.S. global power.
