4. The Bab el-Mandeb strait - 

The Bab el-Mandeb Strait is a narrow maritime chokepoint between Yemen and the Horn of Africa (Djibouti and Eritrea) that links the Red Sea and Suez Canal with the Gulf of Aden and Indian Ocean. Its narrow width—roughly 16 to 32 km (10 to 20 miles) across, split by Perim Island into two channels—makes global trade, energy supplies, and regional security heavily vulnerable to disruptions there

• Global trade and shipping: It serves as a major artery between Asia, the Middle East, and Europe via the Suez Canal. Blocking or avoiding it forces ships to reroute around Africa's Cape of Good Hope, adding roughly 10 to 20 days to voyages and driving up fuel, freight, and insurance costs.
• Energy and supply chains: Large volumes of oil, natural gas, consumer goods, and industrial components pass through the strait. Disruptions affect energy markets and supply chains—including electronics and semiconductors—across Europe, Asia, and the Middle East.
• Undersea communications: Undersea fiber-optic and power cables run through the narrow passage, making it vital for global data connectivity and intelligence monitoring.
• Geopolitical and military flashpoint: Because vessels must pass close to nearby shores and islands, armed groups and regional powers can threaten shipping with missiles, drones, or naval blockades. Nearby Djibouti hosts multiple foreign military bases to monitor the strait and protect maritime traffic. In 2026, renewed Houthi blockades, captures of coastal and island positions like Mokha and Perim Island, and advances toward Aden have heightened security risks around the waterway


