6. Danish Straits.
The Danish Straits—primarily the Øresund (The Sound), Great Belt, and Little Belt—are vital maritime chokepoints connecting the Baltic Sea to the North Sea and the Atlantic Ocean. They serve as the main sea gateway for Northern European commerce, energy shipments, and naval access to the Balt


Trade and Shipping

• High Vessel Volume: Roughly 70,000 to 75,000 ships pass through the Great Belt and Øresund annually, supporting regional and global trade.
• Channel Specialization: Øresund offers a shorter route and serves major hubs like Copenhagen and Malmö, while the deeper Great Belt carries larger vessels and tankers. The Little Belt handles mostly local traffic and serves as a storm alternative.



Energy Security

• Oil Transit Chokepoint: The straits are a major maritime corridor for crude oil and petroleum products moving out of Baltic ports, including Russian seaborne exports.
• Environmental Risk: Heavy tanker traffic—including aging vessels—raises concerns over oil spills and maritime safety in narrow, busy waters.



Geopolitics and Security

• Baltic Access: Any vessel moving between the Baltic Sea and wider Atlantic waters must pass through or near these straits, making them central to regional security and naval monitoring for Denmark, Sweden, Germany, and NATO allies.
• International Passage Rules: The 1857 Copenhagen Convention established free commercial passage through the straits, shaping how coastal states balance transit rights with security and environmental oversight.
